From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexandre Derumier <aderumier@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] quartz: Don't stop a graph run since StopWhenReady().

When the graph is not cued at once, StopWhenReady() leaves the stop to a
thread pool callback, which waits up to 100 ms for the pause to complete
and then stops the graph unconditionally. A Run() called meanwhile does
not cancel it: the graph is stopped under the application, which thinks
it is playing.

Silent Hill: The Arcade (Konami) renders each movie, cues it with
StopWhenReady() and runs it right away: the callback stopped the graph
about a millisecond after the Run(), and every movie stayed black (its
audio and subtitles, driven by the game, went on).

Check the graph is still paused before stopping it: a Run() or Stop()
issued since StopWhenReady() supersedes it.
---
diff --git a/dlls/quartz/filtergraph.c b/dlls/quartz/filtergraph.c
index 5f7c2ae7..2c6dec55 100644
--- a/dlls/quartz/filtergraph.c
+++ b/dlls/quartz/filtergraph.c
@@ -2397,12 +2397,26 @@ static HRESULT WINAPI MediaControl_get_RegFilterCollection(IMediaControl *iface,
 static void CALLBACK wait_pause_cb(TP_CALLBACK_INSTANCE *instance, void *context)
 {
     IMediaControl *control = context;
+    struct filter_graph *graph = impl_from_IMediaControl(control);
     OAFilterState state;
+    BOOL paused;
     HRESULT hr;
 
     if ((hr = IMediaControl_GetState(control, 100, &state)) != S_OK && hr != VFW_S_STATE_INTERMEDIATE)
         ERR("Failed to get paused state, hr %#lx.\n", hr);
 
+    /* Run() or Stop() called since StopWhenReady() supersede it: a graph run meanwhile keeps
+     * running (Silent Hill: The Arcade runs its movie right after StopWhenReady()). */
+    EnterCriticalSection(&graph->cs);
+    paused = graph->state == State_Paused;
+    LeaveCriticalSection(&graph->cs);
+    if (!paused)
+    {
+        TRACE("Graph %p left the paused state, not stopping it.\n", graph);
+        IMediaControl_Release(control);
+        return;
+    }
+
     if (FAILED(hr = IMediaControl_Stop(control)))
         ERR("Failed to stop, hr %#lx.\n", hr);
 
